From fay_hughes at yahoo.co.uk  Mon Apr  2 09:33:34 2001
From: fay_hughes at yahoo.co.uk (Fay Hughes)
Date: Mon, 02 Apr 2001 10:33:34 +0100
Subject: SUMMARY: PRESS statistic in Multiple Regression
Message-ID: <20010402093334.28835.qmail@web4001.mail.yahoo.com>

Firstly, thanks to everyone who replied to my plea for
help, the code helped me immeasurably. Their
summarised responses are below:

1. Peter Ellis <peter_f_ellis at yahoo.com>
There doesn't seem to be an obvious way to do this.  I
make you a present of the following rough and ready
functions which seem to do it although I haven't
thoroughly checked them (and don't know how they'll
cope with NAs, etc).  The function press(x, y) gives
the press statistic for any particular regression;
press.leaps(x, y) effectively works as an enhanced
version of leaps giving the PRESS statistic for all
subsets of x predicting y, albeit by using a clumsy
for loop that effectively repeats all the regressions 
done in FORTRAN by leaps.  Use at your own risk!

press <- function(x, y){
reg <- lsfit(x, y)
levs <- ls.diag(reg)$hat
delres <- reg$resid/(1-levs)
sum(delres^2)}

press.leaps <- function(x, y){
leaps.object <- leaps(x, y)
w <- leaps.object$which
subsets <- dim(w)[1]
out <- numeric(subsets)
for (i in 1:subsets){
preds <- x[ ,w[i,]]
out[i] <- press(preds ,y)}
list(leaps.object=leaps.object, press=out)}

2. Greg Snow <snow at fisher.byu.edu>  
Here are 2 functions, press computes the press
statistic for an x matrix and y vector.  press.leaps
takes the same x matrix and y vector that you
put into leaps plus you give it the "which" matrix
from the leaps function, it then returns a vector of
the press statistics for each of the models (in the
same order as the results of the leaps function).

press.leaps <- function(x,y,which){
        apply(which, 1, function(w,x,y){ press(x[,w],y) },
x=x, y=y )
}

press <- function(x,y){
        ei <- lsfit(x,y)$residuals
        hi <- hat(x)
        sum( (ei/(1-hi))^2 )
}

3. Franz Mueter <fmueter at sfu.ca>  
Allen's PRESS criterion is equivalent to leave-one-out
cross-validation and for multiple regressions can be
computed without refitting each model n times. This is
because it can be shown that for least-squares 
regression 
y_i - y^_(-i)  =  (y_i - y^_i) / (1 - h_ii)
where the y_i are the observed responses, y^_(-i) are
the predicted values for the ith observation based on
a fit to the remaining n-1 observations, y^_i are the
fitted values, and h_ii are the diagonal elements of
the hat matrix H = X(X'X)^(-1)X' (where X is the
design matrix with 1s in the first column and the
independent variables in the other columns). 

Given a model fitted by lm() in S-Plus you can easily
compute the CV(1) or PRESS criterion as follows:

y.hat <- fitted(fit)     # where fit is a fitted
object of class lm
hat.ii <- lm.influence(fit)$hat
CV1 <- mean(((y - y.hat) / (1 - hat.ii))^2)     # y is
a vector of responses
# I believe the PRESS criterion is defined as:
PRESS <- sum(((y - y.hat) / (1 - hat.ii))^2)
 
Thanks, once again.
Fay Hughes

