From adrian at maths.uwa.edu.au  Wed Apr  4 04:09:56 2001
From: adrian at maths.uwa.edu.au (Adrian Baddeley)
Date: Wed, 04 Apr 2001 12:09:56 +0800
Subject: Re: Area inside convex hull
In-Reply-To: <24E34B7B296AD411815300805F578D94B42461@elnte4.nam.dow.com>
References: <24E34B7B296AD411815300805F578D94B42461@elnte4.nam.dow.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.SGI.3.95.1010404114433.21803B-100000@zootje>

> Does anyone have code (or pseudo-code) to calculate the area inside a convex
> hull (chull) or polygon ?  I seem to remember a query on this general
> subject about a year ago, but cannot find it in the archive.  Any pointers
> or code would be appreciated.

The area inside an *arbitrary* closed polygon with vertices 
v_1, ..., v_n with v_n = v_1 is the sum, over all edges e_i =
(v_i,v_{i+1}), of the signed area of Q(v_i, v_{i+1}), the 
quadrangle enclosed by v_i, v_{i+1} and the projections of these
two points onto the x-axis. (The "discrete Stokes theorem")

It's easiest if the vertices are listed in anticlockwise
order around the polygon, and the x-axis is taken to be below
the entire polygon. Then the sign of the quadrangle area is
positive iff the x-coordinate of v_i is larger than the x-coordinate
of v_{i+1}. 

