Message-ID: <2B7F04539DAFD311B61D009027D0DDA20C3DF6@roper-cv.qld.cmis.CSIRO.AU>

> From:         Wing, Michael [mailto:Michael.Wing at orst.edu] 
> Sent: Thursday, 5 April 2001 2:41
> To:   s-news at wubios.wustl.edu
> Subject:      SUMMARY and additional related question: [S] Regression
Trees- In dependent Variable Collinearity
> 
...
> The summary:
> Both responses indicated that regression trees are not robust to
independent
> variable collinearity.
> 
> Brian Ripley wrote:
> Not at all.  Tree-based methods are not robust to many things, which is
why
> methods such as bagging and boosting have arisen.  They were designed to
> find fairly complex but clear-cut relationships.
> 
> Bert Gunter wrote:
> More than "not at all", I'd say: Decidedly ambiguous. The tree topologies
> (though not necessarily the predictions) can change radically with minor
> alterations to the data.
> 
> Additional question:
> Given this lack of robustness, how would one examine and address
> collinearity between a mix of continuous and factor independent variables?

Tree models are inherently non-linear and collinearity, per se, is not an
issue for them in the same way as it is for models that use linear
predictors.

Collinearity is only a source of non-robustness for tree models in as much
as it creates opportunities for alternative splits that perform nearly as
well.  This is a consequence of the greediness of the algorithm and, as
Brian Ripley points out above, is what has led to methods such as bagging
and boosting.  There are even situations where exact collinearity is not
only harmless for tree models but possibly quite a good idea.  For example
if you only had two primary variables, say lat and lon, you might consider
using four derived variables: lat, lon, lat+lon and lat-lon.  This allows
the algorithm to consider cuts at 45 degrees to the major axes as well as
parallel/perpendicular to them and this may produce a much sharper and more
economical classification tree, for example.  (It would be nice if
partition.tree() could be extended to display it, too!)

Conversely tree models are invariant with respect to monotone
transformations of their determining variables.  This implies that if one
variable is just a non-linear but monotone transformation of another, such
as x and x^3, say, there is perfect ambiguity in the tree model, but in
principle no problem at all for linear models.

There are some tools in S-PLUS for investigating the consequences of
alternative splits at various points in the tree, namely burl.tree() and
edit.tree().  In my experience these are rather awkward to use and I suspect
few people have done so, but in principle I would regard them as important.
If you want to try them out, do so with caution, and look at tree.screens()
first.

