From fharrell at virginia.edu  Thu Apr  5 17:45:33 2001
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Thu, 05 Apr 2001 13:45:33 -0400
Subject: Re: Removing unused factor levels from a subset of a dataframe
References: <15052.40495.475514.735287@arbres2a>
Message-ID: <3ACCAF3D.F85A92D4@virginia.edu>

My personal preference is to modify [.factor to drop unused
levels by default.  That's why the Hmisc library does this.
[.factor is called by [.data.frame for factor variables,
so for your purposes you would not need any special
commands.

There is a test version of Hmisc and Design libraries for
S-Plus 6.0 for Unix at 
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/Hmisc.html

For now you have use the full S-Plus make
method to install on Unix.  The web page has binaries
for a test version for Intel Linux S-Plus 6.0.

"David Kane - GEODE
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I often have dataframes with many factor variables that are subsets of much
> larger dataframes. Many of the levels of the factors are not present in the
> subset. I would like to remove them. I know from page 14 of V&R S Programming
> that this is straightforward enough to do. But is there a way to do this for
> all the factor variables in a given dataframe in one pass, without knowing
> ahead of time which variables are present and which are factors?
> 
> Here is a simple example:
> 
> > version
> Version 6.0 Release 1 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.6 : 2000
> > testDF <- data.frame(x = as.factor(1:5), y = letters[1:5])
> > testDF
>   x y
> 1 1 a
> 2 2 b
> 3 3 c
> 4 4 d
> 5 5 e
> > subsetDF <- testDF[1:3,]
> > subsetDF
>   x y
> 1 1 a
> 2 2 b
> 3 3 c
> > levels(subsetDF$x)
> [1] "1" "2" "3" "4" "5"
> > subsetDF$x <- subsetDF$x[,drop = T]
> > levels(subsetDF$x)
> [1] "1" "2" "3"
> 
> But, in order to have a line like
> 
> > subsetDF$x <- subsetDF$x[,drop = T]
> 
> I need to know that x is a) in the subsetDF dataframe and b) is a factor. So,
> is the an elegant way to do this (check for being a factor and remove
> non-present levels) for all variables in a given dataframe? Better yet, is
> there a way to change the underlying S+ default that keeps around non-present
> levels?
> 
> Thanks,
