It seems to me that tree based methods explore the relationship between
dependents and predictors by handling interactions and nonlinear pattern in
an automatic way. However, one needs to resort to the concepts like
variable importance if the focus is on the relationships among predictors.

Unstableness is one of the main criticisms on trees, which might be caused
by various sources including collinearity. If it is collinearity that
causes problem here, a potential remedy could be,  first run factor
analysis, or other dimension reduction tools, on the predictors, so that
highly correlated predictors could be combined and compressed and then
input these combinations as new predictors into CART.

Another solution applies to unstable trees caused by all kinds of sources.
First divide the whole data into subsets, (when sufficiently large sample
sizes are available) or bootstrap the data ( when sample sizes are
moderate), run CART on each of the subsets or bootstrap samples. This
leads to competing tree structures. And combining these competing tree
graphs (through random graph theories) will hopefully give a more robust
structure. (see Dr. William Shannon's work.)

Yes, thanks all for the interesting dialog!

Xiaogang Su

                                                                                
                                                
