Mike,
An interesting post -- and the answer is no.  But a couple of thoughts, if I
may:

(1) there are many other resources other than the ones you listed: Spector's
S-PLUS book has lots of neat ways to accomplish tasks, Azola and Harrell's
"An Introduction", Pat Burns's "S Poetry", and so on (sincere apologies to
those I have omitted here).  Electronically, paths to these can all be
discovered by using the S-news archive, or Statlib, etc. 

(2) It would be nice for there to be a "Joy of Cooking" style compendium, or
perhaps a better metaphor is an O'Reilly Nutshell-type book.  (Indeed,
probably starting with the S-news archive would be a great way to help start
such a book).  But I doubt anyone in the S-PLUS community will quit their
day (and perhaps) night jobs -- which would be required to compile such a
work, *and* maintain and improve it as would be necessary.  S/S-PLUS/R are
just too broad and powerful. Many people use them, but these environments
are not in the same economic incentive league as e.g. Perl, VB, etc. as far
as authoring books goes.   Plus, what is one author's view of
idioms/applications/tasks/problems that are "common" and "generalizable" and
welcomed by the masses will be very different from another author's.

Thanks and Best Regards,
Bill
