From a296180 at mica.fmr.com  Thu Apr  5 21:21:44 2001
From: a296180 at mica.fmr.com (David Kane - GEODE <David Kane)
Date: Thu, 05 Apr 2001 17:21:44 -0400
Subject: Summary for Getting factor levels with paste
Message-ID: <15052.57518.522432.963022@arbres2a>

Thanks to Brian Ripley, Leonid Gibiansky, and Bill Dunlap for taking the time
to consider my question. The best answer for me was Professor Ripley's:

do.call("paste", lapply(temp[by.list], as.character))

Bill Dunlap suggested the same thing, and went on to note that:

#########
By the way, using do.call with data frames can lead to surprises because the 
names
on the columns are taken to be the argument names of the function being called.
paste() doesn't have many unnamed arguments (most are ...), but if your data 
frame
had a column called "sep" or "collapse" you would get unexpected results:

        > df<-data.frame(sep="-", x=1, y=2, z=3)
        > do.call("paste", lapply(df, as.character))
        [1] "1-2-3"

The following unname() function will avoid that problem:
        > unname <- function(x) { names(x)<-NULL ; x }
        > do.call("paste", unname(lapply(df, as.character)))
        [1] "- 1 2 3"
##########

Thanks again,

Dave Kane

