Subject: Re: unlisting data frames (summary)
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.03.10104060905350.1416-100000@ahab>

Once again, the list has provided great help.  Thanks to Renaud
Lancelot, Tim Hesterberg, on MacQueen, and Nick Ellis who told me
about do.call().

The original problem:
>Is there an easy and fast way to collapse a list that contains data
>frames into a single data frame?
>
>Using a for loop is very slow, probably because it reads from and
>writes to disk in each pass.  For example, if A is a long list, each
>element of which is a data frame with same sort of data, the desired
>data frame is B, where:
>   B <- NULL
>   for (i in 1:length(A)) B <- rbind(B,A[[i]])

The solution:
B <- do.call("rbind", A)

As I understand it, do.call treats its second argument as a list of
arguments to be passed to the function specified in its first
argument.  That was just what I needed, as I already had a list of
arguments to rbind.

Anne York pointed out that data.frame(A) would cbind the data frames
if they all had the same number of rows.

Thanks again,

Carlisle

