From a215020 at agate.fmr.com  Fri Apr  6 16:21:42 2001
From: a215020 at agate.fmr.com (David Brahm GEODE)
Date: Fri, 06 Apr 2001 12:21:42 -0400
Subject: Truncated normal distribution
Message-ID: <200104061621.f36GLgA08577@arbres1a.FMR.Com>

There were 2 good solutions to Alastair Dempster's <demp2979 at mach1.wlu.ca>
question about generating truncated normal distributions.  In my tests, the
rejection method is faster than the inverse distribution method if the bounds
lie outside +/- 3 standard deviations.  Here's a piece of code that chooses the
method accordingly:

rnbnd <- function(N, mn=0, sd=1, lo=-Inf, hi=Inf, tol=3) {
  if (hi-mn < tol*sd || mn-lo < tol*sd)               # Alan Zaslavsky's method
    return(mn + sd * qnorm(runif(N, pnorm((lo-mn)/sd), pnorm((hi-mn)/sd))))
  x <- rep(NA, N)                                     # Rejection method
  bnd <- function(z, lo, hi) ifelse(z<lo | z>hi, NA, z)
  while (any(q <- is.na(x))) x[q] <- bnd(rnorm(sum(q), mn, sd), lo, hi)
  x
}
                                -- David Brahm (brahm at alum.mit.edu)

