I'm on the other side, a perl programmer trying to learn SPlus. You'll have
to create an SPlus chapter somewhere where you want the scripts to run,
e.g.:

mkdir /var/www/cgi-bin/swork
cd /var/www/cgi-bin/swork
Splus CHAPTER

Once that's done and you've ensured the directory is +rwx (so that SPlus
can write to disk if needed, if your scripts don't require this, leave them
-w) try this in your perl script:

my $sDir = '/var/www/cgi-bin/swork';
my $cmd = "Splus BATCH $splus_cmdfile $splus_outfile";

# Change the active directory to your splus chapter
chdir($sDir);

# And submit the job
!system($cmd) or die "ERROR in Splus run";

Note that using BATCH submits the job immediately into the background. This
leaves you very little control over output from a perl process. An
alternative is to call the interpreter directly on each line in the
incoming script file. This lets you capture the output and handle it purely
in perl. Here's a post that details how to do this:

http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/s-news/Burst/15548

HTH,
- jc

