From mbauer at usc.edu  Sun Apr  8 22:09:14 2001
From: mbauer at usc.edu (Madeline Bauer)
Date: Sun, 08 Apr 2001 15:09:14 -0700
Subject: Summary: Bagging & Boosting (Re: Regression Trees)
In-Reply-To: <4.1.20010405093739.00944ad0@rcf.usc.edu>
References: <2B7F04539DAFD311B61D009027D0DDA20C3DF6@roper-cv.qld.cmis.C SIRO.AU> <4.1.20010405093739.00944ad0@rcf.usc.edu>
Message-ID: <4.1.20010406110701.0093ba30@rcf.usc.edu>

This is a summary of the recent discussion regarding robustness of tree-based
methods. A summary of references is provided in a separate message.

Many people contributed to this informative discussion. Thanks and appreciation
to: Michael Wing, Brian Ripley, Bert Gunter, Bill Venables, Fernando T. Maestre
Gil, Frank Harrell, Michael Conklin, Thomas Stockton, Joseph S. Verducci,
Cesare Furlanello, Xiaogang Su, Andy Liaw, Frank Javorek, Aldi Kraja. I
apologize in advance if I have left anyone out in the cold and/or my
editing has
misconstrued anyone's reply.

Once Over the Forest Lightly: (painting with broad strokes, leaving the
hairs to be split by the experts, and mixing metaphors): (1) Bagging and
boosting are not "stand-alone" like regression trees.  They can easily be
used on many different prediction
methods.  (2) Tree-based models are useful tools for exploratory data
analysis and
can provide simple visual displays leading to increased understanding of the
underlying data. (3) Computer-intensive methods such as bagging, boosting (aka
arcing), and bumping can improve substantially the accuracy of predictions
obtained from regression or classification models (e.g. trees, logistic). 
(4) However, the resulting structure can be difficult to understand.
Breiman also
proposed a method for obtaining a simple tree representor of the (often
inscrutable) structure resulting from combining multiple predictors. This tree
representor is called a "Born Again" tree [see separate summary of references].

Now to the details of the recent discussion. It all began with a question from
Michael Wing, with answers from Brian Ripley, Bert Gunter and Bill Venables.
These messages were followed by a question from  Fernando T. Maestre Gil, with
answers from  Frank Harrell and Brian Ripley. Finally Madeline Bauer posted a
message asking for references for bagging and boosting methods.  

Michael Wing wrote:
>To what extent are regression tree (with a single continuous dependent
variable)
results robust in regards to collinearity or correlation between independent
variables? 
>
Brian Ripley replied:
Tree-based methods are not robust to many things, which is why methods such as
bagging and boosting have arisen.  They were designed to  find fairly
complex but
clear-cut relationships.
> 
To which Bert Gunter replied:
The tree topologies (though not necessarily the predictions) can change
radically
with minor alterations to the data.
>
Bill Venables commented:
Collinearity is only a source of non-robustness for tree models in as much
as it
creates opportunities for alternative splits that perform nearly as  well.
This is a
consequence of the greediness of the algorithm and, as  Brian Ripley points out
above, is what has led to methods such as bagging  and boosting.
>
Then Fernando T. Maestre Gil posted this message: 
I am using S-Plus and the library RPART to built regression trees with 17
explanatory variables. During the pruning stage using cross-validation, the
best
tree that I can get using the 1-SE rule has only one split and two final
nodes, and,
as consequence, explain a relatively low percentage of the variability in
the data.
As I have observed no case like this in literature, I would like to know if
it is
possible, or if it is suggest the presence of some anomaly in the data.
>
To which Frank Harrell replied:
In my experience that is a common occurrence on non-large (50,000 observation)
datasets. [If you] search for "CART" in the bibliographic database on our
web site 
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/rms [there are a few] references where
performance of recursive partitioning did not stack up well against
regression if
you prune the tree back to where it sustains calibration accuracy during
cross-validation. 
>
And Prof Brian Ripley added:
It is rather common, but unsuccessful uses do not get reported often. In
particular,
this often happens if a linear model is appropriate but no one or two
variables are
good regressors.

After reading the above discussion, Madeline Bauer asked:
Question 1: Are bagging and boosting "methods" in the same sense as, say,
regression trees is a method, or are these adjuncts in the sense that bootstrap
methods are adjuncts and can be used in conjunction with many methods?

Question 2: (Request): It would be helpful to have some good references
(searching the Current Index of Statistics yielded 3!) [See separate
message for a
summary of references]
>
Xiaogang Su replied:
It seems to me that tree based methods explore the relationship between
dependents and predictors by handling interactions and nonlinear patterns in an
automatic way. However, one needs to resort to the concepts like variable
importance if the focus is on the relationships among predictors. 
[Instability] is one of the main criticisms of trees, which might be caused by
various sources including collinearity. If it is collinearity that causes
problem here,
a potential remedy could be,  first run factor analysis, or other dimension
reduction tools, on the predictors, so that highly correlated predictors
could be
combined and compressed and then input these combinations as new predictors
into CART. [MB: but it seems to me that this approach might miss two of the
appealing features of tree-based models, (1) the ability to identify
subgroups of
observations defined by different subsets of predictors, and (2)
"collinear" is just
one of many possible forms of association between predictors.]
(Xiaogang Su continues:) Another solution applies to unstable trees caused
by all
kinds of sources. First divide the whole data into subsets, (when
sufficiently large
sample sizes are available) or bootstrap the data (when sample sizes are
moderate), run CART on each of the subsets or bootstrap samples. This leads to
competing tree structures. And combining these competing tree graphs (through
random graph theories) will hopefully give a more robust structure. (see Dr.
William Shannon's work.)
>
Andy Liaw wrote:
Bagging and boosting are very recent methods.  They are not "stand-alone" like
regression trees.  Bagging stood for "Bootstrap AGgregates", which is typically
used on trees, but can just as easily be used on other methods.  Bagging was
invented by Leo Breiman [see http://www.stat.Berkeley.EDU/users/breiman/].
The idea is that you bootstrap the sample, do the prediction (or
classification or
estimation), and at the end, you average the result of the bootstrap[s] to
come up
with the final answer. Note that if you bag a regression tree, you don't
get a tree as
the end product, just the predictions.
Boosting is an iterative re-weighting procedure. Boosting is originally
proposed
by Shapire & colleagues [see http://www.research.att.com/~schapire/boost.html]
At each iteration, each observation is weighted by its  residual from the
previous
iteration.  The user decides how long to keep at it.  At the end, the
prediction is
the weighted average of all the predictions.
>
And Michael Wing wrote:
I have a function that returns the explained variance for an entire tree
object.  I
would like to also return the explained variance for each  split/node of my
tree
object.  I'd also like to return explained variances  through the graphing
options
offered by post.tree. Any suggestions for functions that could accomplish my
objectives would be  appreciated.
