From tgd at cs.orst.edu  Sun Apr  8 22:50:39 2001
From: tgd at cs.orst.edu (Thomas G. Dietterich)
Date: Sun, 08 Apr 2001 15:50:39 -0700
Subject: bagging and boosting
Message-ID: <41-Sun08Apr2001155039-0700-tgd@cs.orst.edu>

    Question 1: Are bagging and boosting "methods" in the same sense as, say,
    regression trees is a method, or are these adjuncts in the sense that
    bootstrap methods are adjuncts and can be used in conjunction with many
    methods?

    Question 2: (Request): It would be helpful to have some good references
    (searching the Current Index of Statistics yielded 3!)

Bagging and boosting are adjuncts that can be applied to any
classification method.  However, they have both been studied primarily
with tree-based models.  

Bagging is very simple:  Draw L bootstrap replicates of your data, and
fit a model independently to each replicate.  Vote the responses of
each model to make an overall prediction.

Bagging is primarily a variance reduction method, so it is very
helpful with tree-based models, because they suffer from very high
variance (and relatively low bias).  This high variance leads to the
instability that other people have mentioned.  The primary reference
is

@article{b-bp-96,
author = {Leo Breiman},
year = 1996,
title = {Bagging predictors},
journal = {Machine Learning},
volume = 24,
number = 2,
pages = {123--140}}

(a version is available from Breiman's web page as well).

Interpreting a "bag" of models cannot be done simply by inspecting
each mode.  Breiman has developed methods for assessing variable
importance.

Boosting acts to reduce both bias and variance.  It is a method for
building an additive classification model stage-wise.  The reasons for
its (empirical) success are still not well-understood.

The primary reference for boosting is 

@InProceedings{fs-enba-96,
  author =       "Yoav Freund and Robert E. Schapire",
  title =        "Experiments with a new Boosting algorithm",
  booktitle =    "Proc. 13th International Conference on Machine
                 Learning",
  publisher =    "Morgan Kaufmann",
  year =         "1996",
  pages =        "148--146",
}

