On Mon, 9 Apr 2001, vito muggeo wrote:

> Hi all,
> I would like to fit simple Poisson regression model for count data.
> However I've several clusters and furthermore I know that some important
> explanatory variable is missing. So I should fit a poisson model with two
> (nested) random effects:
> (1)Random effect for OBS variable, i.e. 1 random effect=1 count, in order to
> take account the missing covariates effect.
> (2)Random effect for CLUSTER variable, i.e. 1 random effect=1 cluster to
> take account the clustering effect.
>
> Only with (1) (and if the number of clusters is small ) I could use the
> neg.bin or glm.nb() functions
> by Venables&Ripley, but this is no the case here.
> I'm thinking to reglm(...,random=CLUSTER,family=neg.bin(theta)), but I never
> used it.
> Do you think it work?

What function is this reglm()?  It's not in S-PLUS!  There is no GLMM
function in S, not least because how to fit such a model is an open
research problem.  (There are approximations, but no way of knowing when
they are adequate.)

> Furthermore theta in neg.bin() function has to be given,
> but I know nothing about it. How can I estimate it in the model?

(On the last point, see Venables & Ripley.)

It's not clear to me why you need a random effect.  Why can you not
just use a fixed-effect factor for the clusters?  Unless the clusters are
tiny or you are interested in the cluster variablility per se, a
fixed-effects model should be adequate for your needs.

