At 5:35 PM -0400 4/5/01, David Kane - GEODE <David Kane wrote:
> Thanks to David Smith, Don MacQueen, Todd Hatfield, Frank Harrell, Sean Keenan
> and Brain Ripley for taking the time to answer my question.
>
> I have ended up using the following incantation at the top of most functions
> (with an arguement df for the input dataframe).
>
>   df <- data.frame(lapply(df, "[", drop = T))
>
>
>
> This seems to work fine. Special thanks to David Smith. Professor Ripley
> suggested something similar:
>
> subsetDF[] <- lapply(subsetDF, function(x) if(is.factor(x)) x[drop=T] else x)

Note, however, that if any other variables are of class character, 
this will have the (probably unwanted) side effect of converting them 
to factors. For example,

>  tst <- data.frame(a=letters[1:5],b=1:5,c=letters[1:5])
>
>  tst$c <- as.character(tst$c)
>  sapply(tst,class)
        a         b           c
 "factor" "integer" "character"
>  tst[] <- lapply(tst, function(x) if(is.factor(x)) x[drop=T] else x)
>
>  sapply(tst,class)
        a         b        c
 "factor" "integer" "factor"
>

This can be avoided by using I(), as in:

>  tst <- data.frame(a=letters[1:5],b=1:5,c=I(letters[1:5]))
>  sapply(tst,class)
        a         b      c
 "factor" "integer" "AsIs"
>  tst[] <- lapply(tst, function(x) if(is.factor(x)) x[drop=T] else x)
>
>  sapply(tst,class)
        a         b      c
 "factor" "integer" "AsIs"
>

<--- snip --->

