From macq at llnl.gov  Tue Apr 10 21:11:00 2001
From: macq at llnl.gov (Don MacQueen)
Date: Tue, 10 Apr 2001 14:11:00 -0700
Subject: exportData "nonsense"
Message-ID: <p05001907b6f925ad16c6@[128.115.153.6]>

Here is yet another annoying behavior for those of us who dislike the 
automatic conversion of character variables to factors when creating 
data frames.

One way to prevent the automatic conversion is to use the I() 
function, which causes character variables to have class "AsIs". 
Variables with class AsIs are not converted to factors by subsequent 
operations on the data frame.

BUT, exportData() doesn't like them.

tmp is a small data frame.

>  sapply(tmp,class)
    loc analyte  units     gindx    mindate
 "AsIs"  "AsIs" "AsIs" "numeric" "timeDate"

>  exportData(tmp,'outfile',type='ascii',delimiter='\t',rowNames=T,quote=F)
Problem in writeNextDataRows(dh, data): Column number 1 has the class 
"AsIs", which cannot be exported as is. It is best to convert all 
columns to one of the primitive modes (integer, double, character) or 
to class timeDate before exporting the object.

Use traceback() to see the call stack

I note with amusement the statement that "class 'AsIs' cannot be 
exported as is"

-Don
