I think you mean you would like to generate the binary variables
corresponding to a factor.

There are several tricks for doing this. Here's one:

> f <- factor(sample(letters[1:3], 20, rep=T))
> table(f)
 a b  c 
 5 4 11
> outer(as.character(f), levels(f), "==") + 0
      [,1] [,2] [,3] 
 [1,]    0    1    0
 [2,]    1    0    0
 [3,]    0    0    1
 [4,]    0    0    1
 [5,]    0    0    1
 [6,]    0    0    1
 [7,]    0    1    0
 [8,]    1    0    0
 [9,]    0    0    1
[10,]    0    0    1
[11,]    0    0    1
[12,]    1    0    0
[13,]    0    0    1
[14,]    1    0    0
[15,]    0    0    1
[16,]    1    0    0
[17,]    0    0    1
[18,]    0    1    0
[19,]    0    0    1
[20,]    0    1    0

That gives you a matrix with the binary variables as columns.  There are
several ways to turning this into separate variables, but probably the best
is just to make it a data frame.

Bill Venables.

