From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Thu Apr 12 15:45:37 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Thu, 12 Apr 2001 16:45:37 +0100
Subject: Re: splitting strings in S
In-Reply-To: <3AD5C53A.C41284A0@asml.nl>
References: <3AD5C53A.C41284A0@asml.nl>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.31.0104121641050.1560-100000@toucan.stats>

On Thu, 12 Apr 2001, Sicco Schets wrote:

> Dear Mr. Ripley,
>
> (by the way I am using Splus for UNIX-solaris version 6)
>
> What I wanted is a function that searches for a pattern in a string of text 
> (mode
> character) and then splits the string at that character (possibly in- or
> excluding the character), thereby giving as output a vector (or list) with the
> partial strings. It should be optional to allow the string to be split 
> multiple
> times.
>
> An example is the Perl command 'split' or, in the matching part of the 
> function,
> /<pattern>/g, where the g stands for 'multiple instances'.

Right, strsplit in R does that, unpaste in S-PLUS 6.0 does not: it can
only split on a sinlge character not on a pattern.

You can make use of regMatchPos plus substring to do this.

