The basic question is, how do I do post-hoc multiple comparisons of 
fixed effects in a simple linear mixed effects model that has 
interactions?

I have a way using lme() and multicomp(), which is, for example,

  > fmlti <- lme(log(kd) ~ trt -1, data = kd, random =  ~ 1 | soil/trt)

  > multicomp( fixef(fmlti), fmlti$varFix)

and I'd appreciate comments on whether it's right.

Thanks
-Don

(I think the rather long-winded details will help illustrate my question)

I have measurements for Kd, a property of soil. Kd varies greatly for 
different types of soil.

I have 5 treatments (fixed effects) applied to the soil and I want to 
find out if the treatments have an effect on Kd.

I have samples of 10 types of soil, and I want to generalize from 
these samples to the larger population of all soil of those types.

I have replications, that is, measurements on two samples of each 
soil type for each treatment, a total of 2*10*5 = 100 measurements.

Considering the soil samples as random samples from the soil 
populations, lme() appears to be appropriate:

   fml <- lme(log(kd) ~ trt, data = kd, random =  ~ 1 | id)
(at which point model diagnostics look good)

>  anova(fml)
            numDF denDF  F-value p-value
(Intercept)     1    86  6.06130  0.0158
        trt     4    86 12.22201  <.0001

Now, I know that the experimenter will ask which treatments are 
different than which treatments. So I need something like multicomp().

Here's the first thing I tried:
