Does leaps have a bug when dealing with weighted data?

I'd be interested in any responses to Fay's question about how leaps works
with weighted data.  Of course, you can effectively do the leaps procedure
yourself in S+, calculating your own r2 or whatever goodness of fit
statistic you want.  See below where I do this from an lsfit object, and
also compare with the r.squared figure given by summary.lm.  Am I (and
summary.lm) calculating r2 properly for weighted data?

My weighted r2 formula from the lsfit residuals gets very close to the same
answer as from summary.lm, but these figures differ lots from the estimate
of r2 provided by leaps when weights are given.  Specifically, leaps seems
to give much too big an estimate of r2, when the data are weighted.  Does
anyone know why?  is this a bug in leaps' calculation of r2, or in lm and my
calculation? I await advice from wiser heads with interest.  One point - if
the response data is centred around zero instead of (as in my simulated data
below) distant from zero, the problem with leaps seems much less pronounced.

# create some data:
set.seed(0)
x1 <- rnorm(100)
x2 <- runif(100)
w <- 1/(rchisq(100,1)+1)
y <- 20 + runif(100) + (3+rnorm(100))* x1 + (2+rnorm(100))*x2
my.data <- data.frame(y=y, x1=x1, x2=x2, x3=x1*x2, w=w)
rm(x1, x2, w, y)
attach(my.data)

# create leaps with weighted and unweighted data:
my.leaps.w.r2 <- leaps(cbind(x1, x2, x3), y, w, method="r2")
my.leaps.r2 <- leaps(cbind(x1, x2, x3), y, method="r2")

# do lm and lsfit models:
models <- my.leaps.r2$which
out.lm <- out.lm.w <- out.ls <- out.ls.w <- list()
for (i in 1:dim(models)[1]){
out.lm.w[[i]] <- lm(y ~ cbind(x1, x2, x3)[,models[i,]], data=my.data,
weights=w)
out.lm[[i]] <- lm(y ~ cbind(x1, x2, x3)[,models[i,]], data=my.data)
out.ls.w[[i]] <- lsfit(cbind(x1, x2, x3)[,models[i,]], y, wt=w)
out.ls[[i]] <- lsfit(cbind(x1, x2, x3)[,models[i,]], y)}

# compare r2 for lm, lsfit and leaps:
x <-
rbind(
unlist(lapply(out.lm.w, function(x)
{summary(x)$r.squared})),
unlist(lapply(out.ls.w, function(x)
{1-sum(w*x$residuals^2)/sum(w*(y-mean(y))^2)})),
my.leaps.w.r2$r2/100,
unlist(lapply(out.lm, function(x)
{summary(x)$r.squared})),
unlist(lapply(out.ls, function(x)
{1-sum(x$residuals^2)/sum((y-mean(y))^2)})),
my.leaps.r2$r2/100
)
dimnames(x) <- list(c(paste("Weighted", c("lm", "ls", "leaps")),
paste("Unweighted", c("lm", "ls", "leaps"))), 1:7)
round(x, 3)

which provides the following output for different estimates of r2:

