Subject: efficient subsetting
Message-ID: <01C0C739.669F40B0.drobinson@essa.com>

Hello -

I have a perplexing problem and would appreciate any insights.

Background//
I've been asked by a colleague to develop scripts to automate the visual 
display of results from a bootstrapping exercise. The level of aggregation 
required by the end-user is not known in advance, and my scripts work by 
providing, among others, an argument that is a list of boolean flags 
indicating which categorical variables they may wish to aggregate across, 
while preparing a boxplot, histogram or CDF.

I developed the scripts using a test data frame containing ~104,000 total 
observations and 720 bootstraps. At the core of the subset extraction there 
an expression (see Item 1// below). In this example, 'bst' is a vector of 
unique values for the bootstrap number (i.e. it has length 720) and tagX <- 
"CATCH"

On my test data set the worst-case extraction (all graphs are requested for 
all permutations found in the data) produces a swarm of graphs and runs to 
completion in about 3 hours (S-Plus 2000, R3; NT 4.0, 866 MHz CPU, 256 MB 
RAM with no apparent disk thrashing). I delivered the scripts to my 
colleague (same S-Plus; Win 2000, 1,000 MHz CPU, 512 MB RAM with no 
apparent disk thrashing) for processing a full database containing 450,000 
observations and 1,500 bootstraps. I expected it would scale up with some 
penalty, but he found that it had only completed about 75% after 17 hours.

If the full run took 23 hours, that would be about 8 times longer than my 
test data set, with a data set that is 4 times larger on a machine that is 
supposedly faster.
The >2-fold  performance drop was very surprising to me, and I can't think 
of a more efficient approach. I tried first allocating the array that 
collects the results, with no improvement to the logical subsetting 
approach. In fact, the performance went down.

Notes//
In the code snips below, 'db5' is the dataframe, 'bst' is the vector of 
bootstrap numbers, 'tagX' is the string "CATCH", and 'xv.02' is the vector 
to which the assignment (a sum) is made. The 'db5' dataframe has already 
been subsetted by other categorical variables by the time this final 
subsetting is done. The dataframe has these column labels:
------
> dimnames(catch)[[2]]
[1] "ESTIMATEID" "BOOTSTRAPN" "YEAR"       "WEEK"       "MANAGEMENT" 
"GEARTYPEID" "SPECIESID"  "CATCH"
>
------

1//
Here is the first approach, based on a logical comparison (11 seconds to 
first query result).

xv.02 <- c()
for(s in seq(along = bst)) {
   x <- sum(db5[db5[, "BOOTSTRAPN"] == bst[s], tagX)
   if(!is.na(x)) xv.02 <- c(xv.02, x)
}

2//
Here is the second approach (28 seconds):

xv.02 <- rep(NA, length(bst))
for(s in seq(along = bst)) {
   xv.02[s] <- sum(db5[db5[, "BOOTSTRAPN"] == bst[s], tagX])
}
xv.02 <- xv.02[!is.na(xv.02)]

What I'd like to know is if there are any better approaches to (or 
references to) extracting from a fairly large S-Plus database. For example, 
would removing unused columns in the data frame prior to subsetting result 
in a reduction in extraction/query-time? Is it possible to use the by(...) 
to achieve faster results? (If so, how is such a calculation carried out?) 
Finally, is the difference in OS - NT 4.0 vs. Win 2000, significant?

