From swisdom at arbitrade.com  Tue Apr 17 22:26:17 2001
From: swisdom at arbitrade.com (Steve Wisdom)
Date: Tue, 17 Apr 2001 17:26:17 -0500
Subject: Date serial-numbers: Coordinating S+ with my Dilbertware
Message-ID: <C5E03A90949ED3118E7900A0C99D901F2E8886@pluto.arbitrade.com>

I`ve long wondered why S+ date-serials differ by 2 from Excel`s.  For
example, c(4,17,2001) is 36996 in S+ but 36998 in Excel

Having found the answer, I`ll pass it along to save others the effort

. . 

One day is accounted for because Excel treats c(1,1,1900) as day=1 whereas
S+ treats it as day=0

The second results from MSFT`s handling of the imaginary day c(2,29,1900)

I created a dataframe `datesxls` of Excel serial-numbers . .

> datesxls[1 + julian(2, 28, 1900, c(1, 1, 1900)),  ]
 Excel    Y M  D 
    59 1900 2 28

but . . 

> datesxls[1 + julian(3,  1, 1900, c(1, 1, 1900)),  ]
 Excel    Y M  D 
    60 1900 2 29

MSFT says: 

However, in order to be compatible with other programs, Microsoft Excel
treats the year 1900 as a leap year. 

http://support.microsoft.com/support/kb/articles/Q118/9/23.ASP

. . 

